The pill is now solid brand color instead of a neutral outline.

```1:14:apps/frontend/src/components/ui/last-used-pill.tsx
export function LastUsedPill({ className }: { className?: string }) {
	return (
		<span
			className={cn(
				'rounded-full bg-primary px-2 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-medium leading-none text-primary-foreground ring-2 ring-background',
				className,
			)}
		>
			Last used
		</span>
	);
}
```

I used the `primary` pair rather than `violet` directly. They're the same value in the default light theme, but `primary` has a matching `primary-foreground` defined for dark mode and, more importantly, `brand-color.tsx` computes both from the customer's brand color on white-labelled instances — so the label text keeps its contrast instead of being locked to white on whatever color they pick.

The `ring-2 ring-background` matters for the floating placement: it cuts a clean gap around the pill where it straddles the provider button's top border, so the border line doesn't run into the badge. It's a no-op for the inline pill next to the email option.

Lint passes.

